      "idea": {
        "approach": "Implemented two context memories with uncertainty-discounted updates and gradual, per-entry consolidation. A matched control disables consolidation. The required offline tests passed, including a test that consolidated entries change less after contradictory observations and remain frozen when learning is disabled.",
        "falsification": "On paired streams, compare the candidate with the matched control for B recall and switch accuracy. If consolidation does not reduce memory drift or improve recall, the proposed protection mechanism is unsupported; if it slows switches enough to cause any required mean accuracy to trail the mixture by more than two percentage points, it fails the campaign criterion.",
        "hypothesis": "Gradually reducing plasticity for transition entries with accumulated assignment evidence may protect established rules from interference while retaining enough learning capacity to adapt. Earlier restoration results suggest memory drift can contribute to forgetting, but do not establish that online consolidation will help.",
        "limitations": "Consolidation may preserve an incorrectly assigned rule or slow adaptation, and accumulated evidence is not proof that an entry is accurate. The mechanism test uses a simple repeated sequence; no development or evaluation comparison was measured.",
        "question": "Can gradual per-entry consolidation improve retention over the mixture while preserving adaptation to hidden rule changes?"
